%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
## Notebooks as Mini Apps
Let's transform this interactive notebook to a mini app, which uses WLJS as a runtime. Here We will parameterize this 3D plot and add export to an STL file:

### Notebook Content

Create our first cell and add it to initialization group

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  InitGroup: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Model = Manipulate[model = RevolutionPlot3D[{Sin[t] +  Sin[y t] /m,
   Cos[t] +  Cos[x t] /m}, {t, 0, Pi},
  RegionFunction -> (Sin[5 (#4 + #5)] > 0 &), Mesh -> None,
  BoundaryStyle -> Black, PlotStyle -> Thickness[.1], MaxRecursion->1, Axes->False, ImageSize->400], {{x,5, "tX"}, 1, 10, 1}, {{y, 5, "tY"},1,10,1}, {{m,5.5,"Scale"},1,10,0.5}, Appearance->None, ContinuousAction->True];

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
:::note
All global variables we define in this notebook will be automatically scoped to it, when we run it as a mini app. There is no need in a context isolation
:::

Now create another one, where we define an exporting function:


%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
:::note
All global variables we define in this notebook will be automatically scoped to it, when we run it as a mini app. There is no need in a context isolation
:::

Now create another one, where we define an exporting function:


%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  InitGroup: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
export := Then[SystemDialogInputAsync["FileSave", {Null, {"STL" -> {"*.stl"}}}],
  Function[path,
    If[!StringQ[path], Return[]];
    Export[path, model];
  ]
];

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
Here we used `SystemDialogInputAsync` instead of `SystemDialogInput`, since the latter is blocking and cannot be used in calls generated by external asynchronous events such as button clicks.

Notebooks exported as mini apps use the output of the very last cell as the main window. To take advantage of better customization, we use a WLX cell as the output instead of a plain Wolfram Language cell.

<br/>

<details>
  <summary>How to export this notebook</summary>

1. Find the top bar
2. Click *Share*
3. Choose *Mini app*
  
</details>


%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Here we used `SystemDialogInputAsync` instead of `SystemDialogInput`, since the latter is blocking and cannot be used in calls generated by external asynchronous events such as button clicks.

Notebooks exported as mini apps use the output of the very last cell as the main window. To take advantage of better customization, we use a WLX cell as the output instead of a plain Wolfram Language cell.

<br /><details ><summary >How to export this notebook</summary>

1. Find the top bar
2. Click *Share*
3. Choose *Mini app*
  
</details>

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.wlx

With[{
  ExportButton = Button["Export", export]
},
  <div class="bg-white p-4 w-full h-full">
    <div class="flex flex-row gap-x-2 justify-between">
      <Model/>
      <div class="mt-4"><ExportButton/></div>
    </div>
  </div>

]

